2 CONT Geschichte der israelit. Kranken-Verpflegungs-Anstalt und Beerdigungs-Gesellschaft zu Breslau, 1726-1926 : Ḥevra ḳadisha, 5486-5686; by Lewin, Louis, 1868-1941. nr 00036942; Publication date 1926; Topics Israelitischen Kranken-Ver
2 CONC pflegungs-Anstalt (Wrocław, Poland), Hospitals, Jews, Dead, Hospitals. https://archive.org/details/b2982591x
2 CONT
2 CONT Geschichte der Juden in Lissa. Hrsg. mit Unterstützung der Gesellschaft zur Förderung der Wissenschaft des Judentums; by Lewin, Louis, 1868-1941; Publication date 1904; Topics Jews. https://archive.org/details/geschichtederjud00le
2 CONC wiuoft
2 CONT
2 CONT Bookseller AbeBooks description of this book, and the author's background in detail;
2 CONT
2 CONT "Quarto. V, [1], 401, [1]pp. Modern blue cloth binding with gold lettering on spine. Scarce monograph on the history of the Jewish community of Lissa, Posen (today Leszno, Poland). Jewish migration to Posen started as early as the
2 CONC  11th century AD (and perhaps earlier), as Jews fled persecution by Crusaders in Germany. Later periods of migration followed anti-Semitic outbursts in Germany in the 12th through 15th centuries. During this time Poland was a have
2 CONC n for Jews, as the Polish crown granted Jews powers of self-government unheard of elsewhere in Europe (even in later years). Starting with the Statute of Kalisz in 1264, the power to settle disputes between Jews (both civil and cr
2 CONC iminal) was granted to Jewish elders. Poland was also one of the first countries to develop a parliamentary system of government, and a separate Jewish legislature, known as the Va'ad Arba or Council of Four Lands, was founded in 
2 CONC 1581. The "four lands" were Great Poland (includes the Posen region), Little Poland, Podolia, and Galicia. The Va'ad Arba lasted until 1764, when it was dissolved by the Polish national parliament (Sejm). Alongside the Va'ad Arba 
2 CONC was the Supreme Rabbinic Tribunal, which met while the Va'ad Arba was in session. The Rabbinic Tribunal heard appeals of disputes from the regional Rabbinic Tribunals. Previous owner's Ex-libris in Hebrew on inside of front cover.
2 CONC  Ex-library stamps on title page. Moderate age-toning along paper margin. Text in German. Binding in overall very good, interior in good+ to very good condition. About the author: Louis Lewin (1868-1941) was born in Znin, Posen, b
2 CONC ut was raised in Frankfurt am Main. He earned a Ph.D. degree at the University of Heidelberg in 1893 for his dissertation on Rabbi Simeon bar Yohai, and obtained rabbinic ordination at the Hildesheimer Seminary in Berlin. While in
2 CONC  Berlin he also studied with Moritz Steinschneider, the noted Judaica bibliographer, at the Veitel-Heine Ephraim'sche Lehranstalt. Lewin followed in his mentor Steinschneider's footsteps and developed a keen interest in collecting
2 CONC  books and manuscripts. After completing his formal education, Lewin returned to Posen where he occupied rabbinic positions in Hohensalza (German, Inowraclaw - Polish), Pinne (Pniewy) and Kempen (Kepno). In 1920 he received life t
2 CONC enure as rabbi of Kattowitz, Oberschlesien (Katowice, Upper Silesia). Posen (Poznan) and Silesia (Schlesien - German, Slask - Polish) were both areas which had alternated for centuries between Germanic and Polish rule. After World
2 CONC  War I, most of Posen and Silesia became part of Poland. Antisemitism in both regions grew, encouraging migration westward to Germany, other parts of Western Europe, and the United States. At the request of his community in Kattow
2 CONC itz, which officially came under Polish rule in 1922, Lewin publicly refuted antisemitic attacks published in a Polish newspaper. He subsequently received an anonymous assassination threat. This event spurred his move in 1925 to B
2 CONC reslau (Wroclaw), a community that was still in Germany at the time. Lewin held many prestigious posts in Breslau: director of a Jewish educational institution called "Rhedigerheim;" the rabbi of the Abraham Mugdan synagogue; an a
2 CONC dviser for the Judisches Museum Breslau; and a member of the editorial board of the Monatsschrift fur Geschichte und Wissenschaft des Judentums. Lewin was a prolific author and wrote extensively about the history of Jewish communi
2 CONC ties in Posen and Silesia. A complete bibliography of Lewin's writings is in Kiryat Sefer, vol. 19 (1943), p. 114-116. In 1939, Lewin moved to Bnai Brak, Israel, where he lived until his death in 1941. Bookseller Inventory # 38629
2 CONC "
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Biographical Portal of the Rabbis of the Steinheim-Instit online Germany: LEWIN, Louis, Dr., Born December 29, 1868 in ¯nin, Posen, Gest. December 22, 1941 in Tel Aviv. Abitur at the municipal high school in Frankfurt a. 
2 CONC M .; 1893-1895 pupil of M. Horovitz, religion teacher at the grammar school and a secondary school in Frankfurt a. M .; University studies in Berlin and Heidelberg, June 27, 1892 doctorate in Heidelberg; Graduated from RS Berlin, 
2 CONC 1895 rabbinical examination. 1895-1897 Collegiate Rabbi on the Bet-Midrash in Hohensalza (Inowrocław),Poznan, July 1897-1905 Rb. in Pinne (Pniewy), Poznan; tried in 1902 to establish a district rabbinate together with Tirschtiegel
2 CONC , Birnbaum, Neustadt b. Pinne and Meseritz. 1899 application in Myslowitz; 1905-1920 Rb. in Kempen (Kêpno), Posen; Board member of the Association for Jewish History and Literature in Kempen; 1920-1924 Rb. in Katowice, Silesia. 19
2 CONC 25-1939 Rb. the Abraham Mugdan Synagogue in Wroclaw; 1925-1930 head of the "Educational Institution for Needy Israelite Children", a Jewish boarding school, and until 1937 the religious school. From 1933 lecturer in Talmudic studi
2 CONC es at the Jewish Theological Seminary in Breslau; Lecturer at the Machsike Torah Society, scientific advisor to the Jewish Museum and 1929 editor of the museum catalog. Important collection of Hebrew literature and community and f
2 CONC amily documents, later donated in part to Yeshiva University, New York. "Louis Lewin was the historian of the Jews in Poland whose religious, social and cultural life he described in his essays and articles" (Schwab, p. 92). Membe
2 CONC r of the Hebrew Literature Association Mekize Nirdamim. 1939 emigrates to Palestine, retires in Bnei Brak. Married to Meta, geb. Fraenkel; the daughter Vera Anita got engaged to the teacher Martin Miloslawer in Königsberg i. Pr .;
2 CONC  one son was Rb. Dr. Daniel Lewin, Yeshiva Univ., New York. Member of the Hebrew Literature Association Mekize Nirdamim. 1939 emigrates to Palestine, retires in Bnei Brak. Married to Meta, geb. Fraenkel; the daughter Vera Anita go
2 CONC t engaged to the teacher Martin Miloslawer in Königsberg i. Pr .; one son was Rb. Dr. Daniel Lewin, Yeshiva Univ., New York. Member of the Hebrew Literature Association Mekize Nirdamim. 1939 emigrates to Palestine, retires in Bnei
2 CONC  Brak. Married to Meta, geb. Fraenkel; the daughter Vera Anita got engaged to the teacher Martin Miloslawer in Königsberg i. Pr .; one son was Rb. Dr. Daniel Lewin, Yeshiva Univ., New York.
