1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 2004 interview (recording): https://soundcloud.com/oregonjewishmuseum/savinarnorm-2004?utm_source=clipboard&utm_campaign=wtshare&utm_medium=widget&utm_content=https%253A%252F%252Fsoundcloud.com%252Foregonjewishmuse
2 CONC um%252Fsavinarnorm-2004
2 CONT
2 CONT Savinar, Norman David 94 06/16/1916 10/19/2010 Norman D. Savinar died peacefully Tuesday, Oct. 19, 2010, of natural causes. Norm was born to Jacob Savinar (who emigrated from Russia) and Bessie Abrams Savinar in Portland where he 
2 CONC resided save for periods in pursuit of an education or in service to his country. At an early age, along with his brother Richard, Norm was engaged in their father's poultry business on Southwest Front Street where the two of them
2 CONC  dressed and delivered chickens to residents of the west side. Meeting his lifelong friend, Robert Gilbert, in kindergarten, they went on to attend Grant High School and then the University of California at Berkeley. Norman, no sh
2 CONC ining athlete, managed to enter UC Berkeley on a soccer scholarship. Three significant things happened while there: first, he met Adele Silver of Los Angeles who would later become his first wife of 37 years; second, he would majo
2 CONC r in philosophy; and third, he would enter into the ROTC program at the urging of a math professor. After graduation his military service included coaching a GI basketball team; commanding an all-African American unit stateside; a
2 CONC nd during World War II, he entered the Intelligence Corps serving in Western Europe for which he received the Bronze Star. After the war, he returned to Los Angeles and was directed to identify communists currently living in the c
2 CONC ity. During this latter assignment he would regularly dine on Sunday night with Adele's family, many of whom were card-carrying communists. Needless to say, he never found any communists to identify in Los Angeles and was proud of
2 CONC  the fact that he retired as a lieutenant colonel. This set the tone for the rest of his life-one filled with irony, hard work, and two loving marriages. Upon his return to Portland with Adele and his first son, Tim, he operated P
2 CONC ortland Ice and Cold Storage with his brother Richard, and began to purchase small parcels of real estate and stocks and bonds. He and Adele led a simple charmed life on Southwest Buena Vista Drive in a Wade Pipes' house as he pla
2 CONC yed golf at the Tualatin Country Club; presided as the president of Temple Beth Israel during the Rabbi Nodel/Rabbi Rose transition; spent time at the family beach house in Tolovana Park; and shared virtually every Thursday and Sa
2 CONC turday night with his best friend, Bob Gilbert, and his wife, Grace. Once, when he needed a passport to travel to Europe with Adele, he approached the City Records Bureau for a copy of his birth certificate only to be told they ha
2 CONC d no records for a Norman Savinar, but they did have records for a Nathan Savinar. Telling this story to his mother, she replied; "Nathan? I named you Nelson!" During his midlife, Norman expanded his real estate holdings, always p
2 CONC aying cash and treating his tenants like family. During this time he also served on the board of Oregon Freeze Dry which eventually supplied the K-rations to American soldiers during the Vietnam War and then later served on the bo
2 CONC ard of Fibertech-a Hood River company that recycled shipping pallets. An obsessive record keeper, he was once audited by the IRS only to find out that they owed him money. At the very beginning of the jogging craze, Norm caught th
2 CONC e fever and made countless rounds on the Multnomah Athletic Club track and up and down the sandy beaches of Tolovana Park. A year after Adele's death in 1978, Norm met Elaine Jaffe Weil and the two were married in 1990. This marri
2 CONC age brought Norm in to a broader family with Elaine's daughters, their husbands, and their children. Elaine shared Norm's love of the beach and collaborated with him on the remodeling of the cabin. Ultimately, they ended up spendi
2 CONC ng three days a week there for many years and hosting the annual New Year's Day party. In fact, Norm's 75th birthday celebration was held at the beach to which he arrived dressed from head to toe in a pink, furry, bunny costume...
2 CONC with ears. Norm's life was a full one. Filled with spirited dinner table debates; a unique sense of humor; the resolution to lead a rigorous life; a penchant for custom-designed clothes; a keen sense of the business world; and fin
2 CONC ally, the fulfillment of the warmth of the people he chose to marry and others, with whom he chose to surround himself. Norm is survived by his wife, Elaine; sons, Tad Savinar and wife, Georgia of Portland and their daughter, Marg
2 CONC aret of Los Angeles; Tim Savinar and his wife, Patricia Unterman of San Francisco and their son, Harry of Los Angeles; stepdaughters, Sharon Weil; Dana and her husband, Bill Hunt and their daughter, Alicia, and son, Bryan; and Mar
2 CONC lene and her husband, Lou Perez; Norm's brother, Richard Savinar; and sister, Shirley Bergman; all residing in Portland. A memorial service will be held at 4 p.m. Tuesday, Oct. 26, 2010, in the main sanctuary of Temple Beth Israel
2 CONC , 1972 N.W. Flanders St., Portland. Remembrances may be made to Congregation Temple Beth Israel and the Cannon Beach Historical Society.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Published by The Oregonian from Oct. 22 to Oct. 24, 2010.
