1 NAME Henry /Chapman/
2 GIVN Henry
2 SURN Chapman
2 _MARNM Chapman
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1809
2 ADDR
3 CITY Great Houghton
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 DEC 1870
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Henry's convict papers say he came from Great Houghton near Northampton.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was one other convict Henry Chapman that arrived in September 1834 aboard "Ellen"
2 CONT
2 CONT Ballarat New Cemetery
2 CONT INDA Section 3 Grave 3
2 CONT William John Kittelty aged 21 days 8/5/1871
2 CONT Henry Chapman aged 61 1/1/1871 Wood Splitter
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Chapman (c. 1809-1870)
2 CONT
2 CONT Description List: CON23/1/1 (Z2544)
2 CONT Indent: MM33/1
2 CONT c. 1809			He was born at Great Houghton, near Northampton.
2 CONT 11 Jan. 1826	Aged 17, he was tried at the Northampton Town Quarter Sessions and sentenced to 7 years’ transportation for stealing a bucket of nails. He had a previous conviction of 14 days imprisonment for stealing at Northampton 
2 CONC and 14 days for getting into a baker’s shop.
2 CONT 10 Apr. 1826		He left London on board the Chapman.
2 CONT 7 Oct. 1826		He arrived in VD Land.
2 CONT 21 Mar. 1827	He was put into a chain gang for 3 months for being absent from his hut at the Austin’s Ferry between the hours of 12 and 1 o’clock on Monday night.
2 CONT 27 Mar. 1827	His sentence in the chain gang was extended for 14 days for disorderly conduct during Divine Service on the previous Sunday.
2 CONT 19 May 1827	He was given 25 lashes for disobeying orders in taking 2 rugs out of the Chain Gang Room and also making away with his trousers.
2 CONT 25 June 1827	He was given 15 lashes for repeatedly going to the hospital without anything being wrong with him.
2 CONT 22 Sept. 1827	He was given 25 lashes for being absent from his gang at Austin’s Ferry for 2 hours.
2 CONT 26 Sept. 1827	While at the Public Works, he was put into a chain gang for 14 days for having cards in his possession for the purpose of gambling.
2 CONT 13 May 1828	He was given 25 lashes at Maria Island and he was to work in irons during the Commandant’s pleasure for having knowledge of the absence of his bedfellow who slept below him and for not making the circumstances known to
2 CONC  his overseer after a burglary.
2 CONT 30 Dec. 1828	He was given 25 lashes for insolence and for not obeying the orders of his overseer at Maria Island.
2 CONT 19 May 1829	He was given 50 lashes for assaulting and beating his overseer George Graham on the evening of the 10th. He was to work in gaol in irons being a spinner at the factory at Maria Island.
2 CONT 12 Oct. 1829	He was given 50 lashes for ill treating the flagellator.
2 CONT 1 Feb. 1830	He was put in a chain gang for 4 months on Maria Island for disobeying orders and for exciting a disorderly spirit amongst the boys in the spinning shop.
2 CONT 28 July 1830	He was given 35 lashes for absconding from Maria Island on Friday week last and for remaining absent until he surrendered to some soldiers at the sand pit on Wednesday last. He was to be sent to Maria Island for 12 mo
2 CONC nths in addition to his present sentence and work on the tread wheel until so sent.
2 CONT 2 Aug. 1830	He was to work in irons until a communication could be had with Major Lord the Commandant respecting his statement which has been taken this day after absconding from the Public Works on Maria Island.
2 CONT 6 Sept. 1830	He was admonished and discharged after making a noise in the yard during Divine Service the day before.
2 CONT 13 Sept. 1830	He was given 25 lashes for being absent in the Barracks the day before.
2 CONT 22 Sept. 1830	He exposed for sale a pair of shoes in the Barrack yard. Put into a chain gang until the Lieutenant Governor decides whether or not he is to be returned to Maria Island.
2 CONT 11 Jan. 1833	According to the Hobart Town Courier he received a Certificate of Freedom.
2 CONT
2 CONT December 1833 Henry was witness to the wedding of Esther Kittelty and James Waller. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 20 Oct. 1834	He married Ann Kittelty in Hobart (2521/1834). She was the daughter of Solomon Kittelty (c.1790 Scotland-1846 Geelong, Victoria) and Ann Miller (c.1794/8 Scotland-1863, Warrenheip, Victoria). Ann Kittelty was born 181
2 CONC 7 at Aberdeen in Scotland and baptised 1818. She arrived in Hobart 14 August 1833 on board the Thomas. Reference?
2 CONT 30 Sept. 1835	Esther Chapman was baptised (Hobart 7020/1836). She married Solomon Kittelty at Geelong, 6 Sept. 1853. She died 16 Sept. 1909 at Warrenheip, Victoria.
2 CONT 1837	Mary Ann Chapman was born. She died 18 October 1837 (Hobart 5006/1837).
2 CONT 11 Jan. 1839	Mary Ann Chapman was baptised (Hobart 262/1839). She married Frederick Burton at Ballarat, 9 October 1855. She was buried 2 October 1884 at Ballarat.
2 CONT 7 Jan. 1841	He applied for a licence to cut timber at Fleurtys Point, north of Castle Forbes Bay. (LSD392)
2 CONT 11 July 1841	He applied for a licence to cut timber at Flight’s Bay. (LSD392).
2 CONT Dec. 1841	He is listed in the Hobart 1842 census living at Petchey’s Bay with his wife and two daughters (Esther and Mary Ann) living in a bark dwelling. They were Methodists.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 23 Mar. 1842	Harriet Chapman was born. She died as a young child.
2 CONT 10 Oct. 1844	Harriet Chapman was baptised (Hobart 2399/1844). She died 19 June 1852, aged 9 (Hobart 1496/1852).
2 CONT 1851		He is listed in the 1851 Huon census. (CEN1/1/108, p.75)
2 CONT 13 Mar. 1852	He left Launceston headed for Melbourne on board the City of Melbourne. Free by servitude 
2 CONT                              (POL220/1/1, p.607)
2 CONT 14 Feb. 1861	Ann Chapman died at the Ballarat Hospital in Victoria.
2 CONT 29 Dec. 1870	Henry Chapman died at Dead Horse near Ballarat in Victoria. He was buried at the Ballarat New Cemetery 1 January 1871.
