2 ADDR
3 CITY Ballarat
3 STAE VIC
3 CTRY Australia
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1 DEC 1824
2 PLAC Surrey hills
2 ADDR
3 CITY London
3 STAE Surrey
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 DEC 1864
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Australia
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Newspaper Ballarat Star, Mr William Dalton, sexton of St. Paul's Church, a member of the Ballarat Fire Brigade, and for many years of respected resident of Ballarat, died suddenly, and under peculiar circumstances at lake Wendoure
2 CONC e on Thursday morning.   At about 11 o'clock he was called upon by some friends to accompnay them on a rowing excursion on the lake.  With these (Frederick Palmer, James and John Edwards and  William Fearey) he proceeded on foot t
2 CONC o Edwards boathouse.  Whilst the boat was being got ready Dalton came behind James Edwards and Palmer as they stood on the jetty and laid his hands on their shoulders.  He immediately fell backwards, but before he reached the floo
2 CONC r of the jetty, which he did on his side, his fall was broken by Palmer.  Dalton groaned a few times, foamed at the mouth and ground his teeth but never spoke, and he expired in about quarter of an hour.  A surgeon was sent for, a
2 CONC nd Mr. T. Hilias came, but Dalton was then quite dead.  The body was conveyed to Dalton's late residence, Bakery Hill and at 4 o'clock an inquest was held by Dr Clendinning at the Earl of Zetland Hotel.  There the circumstances ab
2 CONC ove related were elicited, as also that Dalton was a strictly sober man, and never seemed to have "the sign of drink upon him".  To William Fearey he had been known for some years.  Six months ago Dalton had said to him he was unf
2 CONC it for duties as a member of the fire brigade.  Dalton became a member of the Ballarat Fire Brigade in February 1863 an obtained a leave of absence in consequence of ill health in September last.  His leave was to continue till Ja
2 CONC nuary 1865.  During the afternoon the flag on the staff of the tower of the fire station house was displayed at half mast and the usual monthly practice (occuring the same evening) was deferred on the members assembling. The decea
2 CONC sed who was a hoseman on the brigade leaves a wife and 8 children.  The funeral is fixed to take place on Sunday afternoon at the Ballarat Cemetery.  The Brigade bell will toll from two o'clock to three o'clock and the members wil
2 CONC l turn out the follow the body of the deceased to the grave. As the deceased was a member of one of the Oddfellows Lodges it is expected that many of the members of the order as well as private friends will join the procession.   
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Creswick and Clunes Advertiser 1864  page 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dec 16
2 CONT
2 CONT This morning a man named Dalton, who for some years past has been a sexton to ST Paul's Church, and a member of the Ballarat Fire Brigade, died suddenly when walking near the swamp. I have been unable to give further particulars. 
2 CONC The deceased bore an excellent character and the Fire Brigade flag was hoisted half mast during the afternoon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dec 19
2 CONT
2 CONT The poor fellow Dalton who died suddenly, as referred to in my last letter has left a wife and eight children unprovided for. His remains will be buried tomorrow and there can be no doubt that the funeral procession will be large,
2 CONC  as besides being followed by the members of the Oddfellows Lodge of which we was a member, the Fire Brigade are summoned to be present.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquest Taken at the Earl of Tillaset Hotel in the District of Ballarat aforesaid, the fifteenth of December 1864 in the 28th year of the reign etc before me George Cluct Gentleman a Coroner etc for the District of Ballarat afores
2 CONC aid upon body of William Dalton then and there lying dead. Upon the oaths of Jurors Daniel Grant, Harry Cawway. Good and lawful men of Australia in the said District who being duly sworn in and by what means the said William Dalto
2 CONC n came by his death do say upon their oath that his death took place on the fifteenth instant on the Wendouree parade Ballarat.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Fearey, Blacksmith.  I knew the deceased for some years and about some six months after I knew him he complained of not being well and unfit for the duties of a Fire Brigade Man and he got leave of absence for some months 
2 CONC on that account.  I called on him this morning with two other to go up to the swamp and went at once to Mr Stuarts house and whilst the boat was being got ready the deceased was standing this time with us on the platform and he ca
2 CONC me behind James Stuart and Frederick Palmer and placed his hands on their shoulders and immediately feel backwards and whilst falling Palmer caught him and broke his fall.  Deceased fell on his side and he groaned a few times, foa
2 CONC med at the mouth and I removed his teeth but never speaking and he expired within a quarter of an hour.  A Doctor was sent for and Dr Hillart came who stated he was quite dead.  He was a very sober man. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick Palmer, Cooper.  I have known deceased for about 14 months. I am not postive whether I have heard him complaining of being unwell.  On the morning about 11 o'clock deceased accompained by John and James Stuart and Willia
2 CONC m Fearey called at my place in Mair Street asking me to go with him for a row in the swamp.  I went with them to Stuarts boat house and choose our boat and while it was getting ready the deceased came behind and went to James Stew
2 CONC art and placed one hand on each of our shoulders and immediately after he fell backwards and I partially broke his fall and he expired in about 15 to 20 minutes after. which he had him conveyed to his own residence on Bakery Hill 
2 CONC Ballarat.  He never spoke to us.  He was a very sober man.  I never saw the sign of pain on him.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Tonie Stobson MB, Surgeon.  That I am a legally qualified medical practitioner and I knew the deceased for a number of years but he had not been under my care professionaly.  He always appeared to me to enjoy good health. 
2 CONC  I have this day examined his body and there are not any marks of violence of any kind on any part of his body.  On opening the chest I found some old pleuritis, a hepatious in the left side of chest but both lungs and the heart a
2 CONC nd its appendages were perfectly normal.  On opening abdomen I found the cavity full of fluid blood amounting to a quart or more on examination I found a small tumour attached to the aorta behind and above pancreas which I found w
2 CONC as an anneurism of that portion of the aorta and which was ruptured and the cause of death was the internal haemorrhage ensuing upon the rupture of the anneurism.`
