1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT When Frances Poplack passed away in Bellingham on January 10, 2000, one of the last direct connections to the early Beth Israel families was lost. Her father, Max Poplack, came from the same area of Lithuania as many other early B
2 CONC ellingham families. He arrived here around 1910 to join brothers and assorted cousins that included members of the Bean, Grieff, and Levin families. Within a few years Max and his brother Phillip established Poplack Brothers Groce
2 CONC ry, a landmark institution on Eldridge Street for over 30 years. Max married Lilly Shapiro, another recent arrival from Lithuania, and together they raised four girls in a house on Elm street. Relatives and distant cousins lived o
2 CONC n the same block, and the home was just a few blocks from the synagogue they supported.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although memories of the Poplack family have largely faded, some remnants remain. Lilly Poplack donated a curved metal railing for the new synagogue's front steps, and although much of it was replaced a few years ago, short sectio
2 CONC ns of the original remain up near the door. The support of Phillip and Bessie Poplack (Frances' aunt and uncle) also helped make the current building a reality 75 years ago. Their names appear on one of the cornerstones as a remin
2 CONC der of the Poplack family's contributions to the early Jewish community in Bellingham.
