1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT 11 Jan 1935, Friday, Sunshine Advocate, page 7
2 CONT Wedding Bells
2 CONT Gillies - Kittlety
2 CONT St Mark's Church, Sunshine, on December 22nd, ws the scene of the marriage of Miss Eady Kittlety, daughter of Mr and Mrs Kittlety of Chapman Street, Sunshine, and late of Warrenheip, and Mr Angus George McLean Gillies, son of Mrs 
2 CONC and the late Mr Angus Gillies of Barrow Street, Brunswick.  The Rev. L J Harwood was the officiating clergyman.
2 CONT The bride wore a frock of magnolia satin with godets from the knees, long tight fitting sleeves, puffed elbows and tight fitting bodice, a honitan lace veil was worn, surmounted by a coronet or orange blossom and the bouquet was a
2 CONC  sheaf of water lilies.   The ensemble was completely by neat white kid shoes. 
2 CONT Miss Mat Harris of Dunnstown, and Miss Jean Murray of Sunshine were the bridesmaids. 
2 CONT They were both attired in blue floral georgette, full length with cape sleeves, and carried bouquets of delphiniums and carnations.  A dainty trainbearer was Rayma Huxtable, of Coburg (niece of the bride), in a pretty frock of lem
2 CONC on georgette, Early Victorian style.  She carried a basket of water lilies and delphiniums.
2 CONT At the church the organ was nicely played by Miss Grace Dean, and Mr Allen Sainty sang the solo, "Because".  The best man was Mr Ernest Pearce of Brighton, and Mr Jack Kittlety (brother of the bride) acted as groomsman.
2 CONT The bride's mother wore a navy blue ensemble and carried a bouquet of red carnations and fern, and the bridgegroom's mother chose ice blue georgette for her ensemble and pink carnations and ferns for her bouquet. 
2 CONT The reception was held at the Masonic Hall where the usual toasts were honored. 
2 CONT The toasts to the newly-weds was proposed by Mr G Thomas, while that to the bridesmaids was proposed by the Rev Mr Harwood.  During the evening songs were rendered by Mr Sainty and Mr Deely and a violin solo by Mr Gordon Hart, the
2 CONC  piano accompaniments being played by Mrs Faulkiner.
2 CONT The bride's travelling costume was of dove grey, with a blouse of pink.  Lakes Entrance was chosen for the honeymoon and the happy couple's residence will be at Brunswick.
2 CONT The bouquets were prepared by Mr Deely, and Mrs Hebdon attended to the tables and decorations for the reception.
