1 NAME John William /Skinner/
2 GIVN John William
2 SURN Skinner
2 _MARNM Skinner
1 SEX M
1 OCCU John Skinner was born in Lincolnshire, England in 1833. He arrived in Hobart with his father, Joseph, at the age of 9 and they settled in the Huon district in 1843 to pursue a life of farming. When his father died, John Skinner ca
2 CONC me in to possession of the "Clifton Grove" property, which consisted of 96 acres, 10 of which were laid down in orchard. |Besides farming, he worked a quarry for quite a few years, and was a part owner of a sawmill, which he carri
2 CONC ed on for twelve years. John Skinner was the chairman of the Road Trust for a number of years and at one point was on the committee of management of the Congregational Church. He married Rosetta Lomas daughter of John Lomas of Por
2 CONC t Esperance in 1868 and they had a family of nine.
2 DATE DEC 1842
2 ADDR
3 CITY Hobart
3 STAE Tasmania
3 CTRY Australia
1 BIRT
2 DATE 25 AUG 1833
2 PLAC Barton upon Humber, North Lincolnshire, England
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE DEC 1921
2 PLAC Huonville Congregational Cemetery, Huonville, Tasmania, Australia
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 21 DEC 1921
2 ADDR
3 CITY Huonville
3 STAE Tasmania
3 CTRY Australia
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Examiner Launceston Tas Wednesday 2 March 1921
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fire Fiend Hobart, Tuesday, The residence of Mr John Skinner of Clifton Grove and old and well known homestead lying between Huonville and Glen Huon was together with its contents completely destroyed by fire this morning.  Th
2 CONC e insurances will not cover half and damage done. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mercury Hobart Tas Wednesday 2 March 1921
2 CONT Fire at Huonville.  Well Known Homestead completely destroyed.
2 CONT Cygent, March 1.  The residence of Mr John Skinner Clifton Grove, an old and well known homestead, lying between Huonville and Glen Huon, was early this morning, together with all its contents, completely destroyed by fire.  About
2 CONC  4 am Miss Vince a maid employed in the house, was awakened by the crackling of fire and the suffocating effect  of dense smoke.  She at once rushed to Mr and Mrs Skinner's bedroom and told them of their danger and then ran outsid
2 CONC e and called loudly for help.  Mr W R Cordwell and his son, who were occupying a hut nearby,  were the first to respond to the alarm, and running to the house, found that half of it was lamost entirely enveloped in flames.  The fi
2 CONC rst anxiety was for Mr Skinner, who is very aged, being about 87.
2 CONT and an invalid and rushing in, they found him being attended by Mrs Skinner who is herself over 80.
2 CONT A minute or so afterwards, Mr William Skinner their son arrived on the scene and carried his father to safety. 
2 CONT The flames spread with alarming rapidity, and before much furniture was removed the fire fighters 
2 CONT had to adandon their efforts.  Only a small portion of the furniture was moved.
2 CONT Up to the present those interested do not seem to be  exactly certain as to the extent of the insurance, but it is believed it only respresents a very small amount, and not sufficient to cover half the damage done.
2 CONT The origin of the fire is not clear, but it is probable that it was caused by a spark from the stove,  as the back was the first part of the house to be consumed.
2 CONT The homestead which was a large one of 13 or 14 rooms was one of the landmarks of the district.
2 CONT Fortunately , there was no wind at the time so that a number of outbuildings containing valuable orchard requisites and other things escaped undamaged.  Great sympathy is felt for the aged couple in their loss.
2 CONT Mr Skinner is of course a well known identity having been one of the sturdiest of the old pioneers,  and also one of the best respected men in the Huon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mercury Hobart Tas Friday 30 December 1921
2 CONT In the death of Mr John Skinner
2 CONT  of Huonville there has passed away one of the last remaining of the original pioneers of the Huon.
2 CONT As he was in his ninetieth year and came with his parents from England to Tasmania when 10 years old, he had seen vast development in the land which had been his home for 80 years, and more particularly in that part of it, the Huo
2 CONC n to which he came from Browns River in his early manhood to carve out a home and in the opening up of which district he was ever to the fore. 
2 CONT He was a man of substance and had enjoyed through life a reputation for integrity and general uprightness.  He was as his sons remain among the leading orchardists of the Huonville district.
2 CONT
2 CONT Something over a year ago he suffered the cruel experience of seeing the house in which he had lived for the greater term of long life burned down over his head almost, for it was which considerable difficulty that he was rescued,
2 CONC  he being ill in bed at the time.
2 CONT The funeral in the Congregational cemetery at Huonville was marked by widespread evidence of esteem. 
