1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com.hr/research/collection-1/myheritage-obiteljska-stabla?itemId=127319401-2-501136&action=showRecord&indId=externalindividual-a8f7592bd766dadc59c344c932110072&mrid=749c823e6fb0
2 CONC 0d2d64c51b85ca1023f4 MyHeritage Family Trees] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 14 2016, 15:20:27 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 July 1889 newspaper, Albert Dalton charged with deserting his wife Eliza 
2 CONT
2 CONT 24 April 1896
2 CONT Albert Dalton is charged on warrant with deserting his wife, Eliza Charlotte Dalton, 50 York street, Ballarat East  (street has possibly been re numbered at some stage)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 Dec 1906 The Herald. Page 3. Care of infants. A nurse fined.  At Brunswick court this morning Mrs Eliza Charlotte Dalton, of 82 (can quite read 82 or 83?)  Albion St, Brunswick, was charged with retaining an adopted infant in h
2 CONC er care without being registered under the infant life protection act.  Defendant admitted the offence. 
2 CONT formerly a registered nurse at East Brunswick.  She removed to West Brunswick and built a residence.  As the Chief Commissioner of Police was not satisfied with the house, he refused to register defendant again.  Senior-Constable 
2 CONC Lonigan gave evidence that he furnished a report to the Chief Commissioner of Police, who issued instructions that defendant should send the child back to the mother. Defendant, when told this, said, "Oh, you cannot take the child
2 CONC ; is is my cousin's, and I have adopted it."
2 CONT Defendant subsequently admitted that the mother was no relation.  She added that she told the falsehood to screen the mother.  The mother of the child deposed that at first she gave defendant 6s per week to look after the infant. 
2 CONT Afterward defendant undertook to adopt the infant for a fee of 6 pound.  This was paid. Mr Lord J P said that it was a serious thing to attempt to defeat legislation expressly framed for the protection of infant life.  A fine of 3
2 CONC  pound, in default one month's imprisionment, was imposed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 13 Dec 1906 The Age Melbourne Page 9
2 CONT An unregistered nurse.  Eliza Charlotte Dalton, a middle aged woman, was charged with having an infant in her care without being registered under the infant life protection act.  Accused pleaded guilty.  Sergeant Percival said acc
2 CONC used had applied for registration, but this had been refused.  When she was found to be keeping the infant in question she declared that she had adopted the child, whose mother was a relation of hers.  She also stated that he had 
2 CONC not received, and was not to receive, any consideration or reward in respect to the care of the child. 
2 CONT Senior-constable Lanigan, of West Brunswick, deposed that the accused lived in Albion street and that when he visited her and questioned her she made the statements aforementioned. 
2 CONT The mother of the infant swore that she agreed to pay 6/ per week for the care of the child, and subsequently agreed to give 6 pound for the adoption.  Defendant said she had been registered for a number of years, but further regi
2 CONC stration was refused, on the ground that her house was not finished. 
2 CONT Mr Lord, J P, said the bench looked on the offence as a very serious matter, and defendant would be fined 3 pound, in default
2 CONT a month's imprisionment. Persons who undertook such grave responsibilities should be careful to comply with the law.
2 CONT Defendant, who tearfully pleaded to be allowed to keep the infant, was told she must give it up or run the risk of further prosecution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Jul 1917 Monday
2 CONT The Age Melbourne
2 CONT Dalton On the 20th June at her residence, No 329 Sydney Road, Brunswick, Eliza Charlotte Nelson, the dearly beloved wife of Charles Dalton, loving mother of Albert and Arnold (on active service), and of Gordon, Norman and Phyllis,
2 CONC  aged 58 years. 
