2 CONT Burial 29 November 1884 Ballarat Old Cemetery D Section Row 2 Grave 14 Dalton's Grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT Newspaper 
2 CONT
2 CONT In memory of James Leslie Magee 
2 CONT
2 CONT who was drowned at Haddon 27th November
2 CONT
2 CONT Hark! how the frantic mother calls, Her first born baby by his name;  She seeks and calls her little dear - Alas! to her no answer came.
2 CONT
2 CONT Away he speeds in painful fright, Dreading to think what may meet her sight -
2 CONT
2 CONT Yes; there in the water her loved one she found.
2 CONT
2 CONT That a short time before was toddling around
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tis better far to lose your  ? , So sweet, so lovely, and so fair,  Than see him grow to gay manhood, Then pine, and wither in a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jesus has called you, little lamb, Across the river, ?
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps fearing that his sheep might stray, And then at last be lost.  Don't grieve for little Jimmy, 
2 CONT
2 CONT ? has only gone before.  ? for you at the Golden Gate, ? bright celectial shore.  Un Amie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Minnie's Sorrow.
2 CONT
2 CONT I am no poet, yet I'll try to write a little Theme of one I know - I know her still - And Minnie is her name
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a bright and winsome lass As you could wish to see - so kind so true, So merry too, and neat as e'er could be.
2 CONT
2 CONT Young James, he won this Minnie, Won hat boon - a loving wife - And happy were they in their home, Nor knew the name of strife.
2 CONT
2 CONT To crown their joy, a lovely boy Was sent to bless their home, An angel fair, with golden hair, His eyes like stars they shone.  lived so happy and so gay, a seemed to have a sorrow; went wrong, then one
2 CONT
2 CONT ? "Well, better luck to-morrow,"
2 CONT
2 CONT ? row to those loved ones came Like thunder's fearful sound, Their loving child, their own sweet boy, James Leslie, was drowned.  Who can depict the scene of woe, As in they brought the child. ?
