2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Solomon Hyams was born in London and died in British Columbia.  He studied anatomy at Harvard, and served as a lay rabbi in British Columbia.  He first came to British Columbia in 1958 then worked in England, Boston, NY, an
2 CONC d San Francisco.  Around 1884, he went back to British Columbia where he worked as an optician.  Samuel Solomon Hyams had six siblings.  __________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Solomon Hyams, a native of London, England, came to Victoria in 1858, at age 17. He was elected Secretary and later Vice-President of the First Hebrew Benevolent Society, and Secretary pro-tem of the cemetery committee. Hya
2 CONC ms left the colony in 1863, returning to England for further education. He then became a lecturer at Harvard University, Cambridge, Mass. After residing in New York and San Francisco, he returned to Victoria with his family. In 18
2 CONC 84, Samuel Solomon Hyams was the proprietor of S.S. Hyams Optician on Fort Street. In 1889, he assisted in the first formal Rosh Hashona services in Seattle. In 1892, he conducted the first public High Holyday services in Vancouve
2 CONC r.
