1 NAME Alan Harold /Selling/
2 GIVN Alan Harold
2 _MARNM Selling
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 NOV 1952
2 ADDR
3 STAE Oregon
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 AUG 2014
2 ADDR
3 CITY Portland
3 STAE Oregon
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Obituary:
2 CONT Alan Selling Obituary
2 CONT Selling, Alan 61 Nov. 20, 1952 Aug. 06, 2014 "Gifted," "gentle" and "generous" defined Alan's life. Alan was blessed with innate musical talent, learning the piano and guitar on his own, and taking lessons on brass instruments. He
2 CONC  enjoyed playing the piano whenever one was available at a friend's house and, for a number of years, played at Nordstrom during the holidays. And, although he never owned a pet, he was more than hospitable to the stray cats who o
2 CONC ccasionally wandered to his house, providing food, shelter and water, allowing them to come and go as they please. His humor comprised many jokes, often repeated over years, that he got from his parents, friends and several joke b
2 CONC ooks. It made him smile to see others smile. This strong inclination to share with others, especially smiles, was why his entire neighborhood looked after him. He adapted his life to his mental illness so he was able to live indep
2 CONC endently and, more important, be self-aware enough to both accept and give help. His neighborhood was like a second family; they accepted Alan's quirkiness, kindness, and occasional requests for help as part of Alan's unique chara
2 CONC cter, and knew him from his daily walks and friendly, brief conversations. Alan, a lifelong Portland resident, grew up in the Parkrose area when it was still a distant suburb, and was on the high school's diving team. After his fa
2 CONC mily moved to the southwest part of the city, he settled in to the Markham neighborhood for the last 30 years of his life. He frequently invited friends over to his house and easily shared whatever he had. He trusted and watched o
2 CONC ut for his neighbors and friends, who, in turn, knew that if he borrowed something, he absolutely could and would pay it back. Alan's life in his house was filled with sharing, conversation, friends, safety, quiet time for himself
2 CONC  and self-reliance to get through each day. His gentle and generous nature extended to everyone who visited him. Alan is survived by his sister, Judith Bullard-Schatzel (Jim) of Portland; brother, Joel Selling (Nancy Truitt Pierce
2 CONC ) of near Seattle; niece, Teresa St. Arnauld (Dwayne); and nephew, Drew Benson Selling. A celebration of Life for Alan will be held at 2 p.m. Saturday, Sept. 6, 2014, at St. Clare Church, 8535 S.W. 19th Ave., in Portland. In lieu 
2 CONC of flowers, his family requests that donations be made, in Alan's name, to the Laurence Selling Research Fund at OHSU. Alan left the world with more laughter, kindnesses and friendliness than when he entered. We would all benefit 
2 CONC from being able to say the same at the end of our lives.
2 CONT To plant trees in memory, please visit the Sympathy Store.
2 CONT
2 CONT Published by The Oregonian from Aug. 15 to Aug. 20, 2014.
