1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===[http://www.nytimes.com/2016/11/12/arts/music/leonard-cohen-songwriting-appraisal.html?_r=0  '''Leonard Cohen, Master of Meanings]
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.tabletmag.com/tag/leonard-cohen  '''Leonard Cohen (1934 - 2016)]
2 CONT *[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YrLk4vdY28Q  '''Hallelujah]
2 CONT *[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v0nmHymgM7Y '''Leonard Cohen’s new 2016 album] 
2 CONT *[http://forward.com/articles/205890/a-song-of-love-and-memory-for-leonard-cohen-at-/?p=1   '''A Song of Love and Memory for Leonard Cohen]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>''.  .  .  “Though I let my heart get frozen,” Cohen sings, “to keep away the rot / my father says I’m chosen / my mother says I’m not / I listened to their story of the gypsies and the Jews / It was good, it wasn’t boring / It 
2 CONC was [http://www.tabletmag.com/scroll/182591/listen-to-the-first-single-from-leonard-cohen almost like the blues].”
2 CONT
2 CONT As for the '''Leonard Cohen's October 1973 Yom Kippur War Sinai Concert''' you may read the About content on the Geni page:
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/Meir-Garbarz-Gover-Halevi-c/6000000034622561977
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonard_Cohen  '''Leonard Norman Cohen, CC GOQ] (21 September 1934 – 7 November 2016) is a Canadian singer-songwriter, musician, poet, and novelist. His work has explored religion, politics, isolatio
2 CONC n, sexuality, and personal relationships.  Cohen has been inducted into the American Rock and Roll Hall of Fame and both the Canadian Music Hall of Fame and the Canadian Songwriters Hall of Fame. He is also a Companion of the Orde
2 CONC r of Canada, the nation's highest civilian honour. In 2011, Cohen received a Prince of Asturias Award for literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT The critic Bruce Eder assessed Cohen's overall career in popular music by asserting that "[he is] one of the most fascinating and enigmatic … singer/songwriters of the late '60s … [and] has retained an audience across four decades
2 CONC  of music-making … Second only to Bob Dylan (and perhaps Paul Simon) [in terms of influence], he commands the attention of critics and younger musicians more firmly than any other musical figure from the 1960s who is still working
2 CONC  at the outset of the 21st century."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Academy of American Poets has commented more broadly on Cohen's overall career in the arts, including his work as a poet, novelist, and songwriter, stating that "[Cohen's] successful blending of poetry, fiction, and music is m
2 CONC ade most clear in Stranger Music: Selected Poems and Songs, published in 1993, which gathered more than 200 of Cohen's poems … several novel excerpts, and almost 60 song lyrics … While it may seem to some that Leonard Cohen depart
2 CONC ed from the literary in pursuit of the musical, his fans continue to embrace him as a Renaissance man who straddles the elusive artistic borderlines."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Cohen was born on 21 September 1934 in Westmount, an English-speaking area of Montreal, Quebec, into a middle-class Jewish family. His mother, Marsha (Masha) Klonitsky, was the daughter of a Talmudic writer, Rabbi Solomon Klonitsk
2 CONC y-Kline of Lithuanian Jewish ancestry. His paternal grandfather, whose family had emigrated from Poland, was Lyon Cohen, founding president of the Canadian Jewish Congress. His father, Nathan Cohen, who owned a substantial clothin
2 CONC g store, died when Cohen was nine years old. On the topic of being a Kohen, Cohen has said that, "I had a very Messianic childhood." He told Richard Goldstein in 1967, "I was told I was a descendant of Aaron, the high priest."
2 CONT
2 CONT Cohen attended Roslyn Elementary School and, from 1948, Westmount High School, where he was involved with the student council and studied music and poetry. He became especially interested in the poetry of Federico Garcia Lorca. As
2 CONC  a teenager, he learned to play the guitar, and formed a country-folk group called the Buckskin Boys. Although he initially played a regular acoustic guitar, he soon switched to playing a classical guitar after meeting a young Spa
2 CONC nish flamenco guitar player who taught him "a few chords and some flamenco."
2 CONT
2 CONT Cohen frequented Saint-Laurent Boulevard where he went for fun, and ate at places such as the Main Deli Steak House. According to journalist David Sax, the Main Deli was where Cohen and one of his cousins would go to "...watch the
2 CONC  gangsters, pimps, and wrestlers dance around the night." Cohen also enjoyed visiting the previously raucous bars of Old Montreal as well as Saint Joseph's Oratory, which had the closest restaurant near Westmount where he and his 
2 CONC friend Mort Rosengarten could go for coffee and a smoke. After moving out of Westmount, Cohen purchased a place in the previous working-class neighborhood of Montreal's Little Portugal on Saint-Laurent Boulevard where he read his 
2 CONC poetry at various surrounding clubs. It is also during his time there in the small neighborhood that he wrote the lyrics to what would become some of his most famous songs. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonard_Cohen .   .   .  '
2 CONC 'Continued]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Awards, titles and honours
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cohen was honored alongside Chuck Berry as the recipients of the first annual PEN Awards for songwriting excellence-at the JFK Presidential Library, Boston, Massachusetts on 26 February 2012.
2 CONT * 1964 the Québec Literary Competition Prize (awarded 1923–70 by the Province of Québec) The Favourite Game (Cohen's first novel)
2 CONT * 1968 Governor General's Award (English language poetry or drama) for Selected Poems 1956–1968. (Refused)
2 CONT * 1970 Honorary degree from Dalhousie University
2 CONT * 1984 The Golden Rose, the main award at the international television festival Rose d'Or in Montreux, for I am a Hotel, made-for-TV short musical film written by Cohen and based on his songs.
2 CONT * 1985 Canadian Author's Association Literary Award for Poetry for Book of Mercy
2 CONT * 1986 Genie Award for Best Original Song for "Angel Eyes" from Night Magic (with Lewis Furey)
2 CONT * 1988 Columbia Records Crystal Globe Award from CBS for I'm Your Man. The award is for artists who sell more than five million copies of an album in foreign territories
2 CONT * 1989 Nominated for Juno Awards for Canadian Entertainer of the Year and Male Vocalist of the Year
2 CONT * 1991 Officer of the Order of Canada
2 CONT * 1991 Induction into the Juno/Canadian Music Hall of Fame
2 CONT * 1991 Nominated for a Juno Award for Songwriter of the Year.
2 CONT * 1992 Honorary degree from McGill University, Montreal.
2 CONT * 1993 Juno Award for Male Vocalist of the Year. The video for Closing Time, directed by Curtis Wehrfritz, won for Best Video. He was also nominated as Producer of the Year (with co-producer Leanne Ungar, for "Closing Time").
2 CONT * 1993 Governor General's Performing Arts Award for Lifetime Artistic Achievement.
2 CONT * 1994 Juno Award for Songwriter of the Year and was nominated for the Juno Award for Album of the Year (for The Future). The video for The Future, directed by Curtis Wehrfritz, was nominated for Juno Award for Best Video.
2 CONT * 1996 Ordained a Rinzai Buddhist monk.
2 CONT * 2001 Nagroda Muzyczna Fryderyk, the annual Polish music award, for best foreign album (Ten New Songs).
2 CONT * 2002 Nominated for Juno Award as Best Songwriter Cohen (with Sharon Robinson) for "Boogie Street", "In My Secret Life", and "You Have Loved Enough").
2 CONT * 2002 Nominated for the Juno Award for Best Video for "In My Secret Life", directed by Floria Sigismondi. Ten New Songs nominated for Best Pop Album, and he was nominated for Best Artist.
2 CONT * 2002 SNEP Award Cohen for more than 100,000 copies sold of Ten New Songs in France.
2 CONT * 2003 Companion of the Order of Canada, Canada's highest civilian honour.[107]
2 CONT * 2004 Inclusion in Canada Reads 2005 with Beautiful Losers
2 CONT * 2005 Induction into the Canadian Folk Music Walk of Fame.
2 CONT * 2006 Induction into the Canadian Songwriters Hall of Fame.
2 CONT * 2007 Grammy for Album of the Year as a featured artist on Herbie Hancock's River: The Joni Letters.[108]
2 CONT * 2008 Induction into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame by Lou Reed who described Cohen as belonging to the "highest and most influential echelon of songwriters.".[109][110]
2 CONT * 2008 Grand Officer of the National Order of Quebec.[111]
2 CONT * 2009 Long Listed for the Polaris Music Prize for Live in London[112]
2 CONT * 2009 Nominated for Mojo Honours Lists for Best Live Act category.[113]
2 CONT * 2009 Meteor Music Award for Best International Live Performance for his 2008 show in Dublin's IMMA's Royal Hospital Kilmainham.
2 CONT * 2010 Grammy Lifetime Achievement Award.
2 CONT * 2010 A second Meteor Music Award for Best International Live Performance for his 2009 show in Dublin's O2.
2 CONT * 2010 Porin Award in Croatia in category of foreign video programme, for his Live in London DVD.[114]
2 CONT * 2010 Induction into the Songwriters Hall of Fame.
2 CONT * 2011 Glenn Gould Prize[115]
2 CONT * 2011 Prince of Asturias Awards, Literature, Spain
2 CONT * 2012 Inaugural PEN Award for Song Lyrics of Literary Excellence. Chuck Berry received same award.[116][117]
2 CONT * 2012 Prix Denise-Pelletier
2 CONT * 2013 Juno Award, Artist of the Year.
2 CONT
