2 CONT ==Film and television
2 CONT * Cohen was the subject of the 1965 documentary Ladies and Gentlemen... Mr. Leonard Cohen, directed by Donald Brittain and Don Owen and produced by the National Film Board of Canada. This film, which pre-dates Cohen's career as a 
2 CONC songwriter, explores his career as a well-known Canadian poet.[121] Cohen also appeared in Owen's 1967 film The Ernie Game, which was entered into the 18th Berlin International Film Festival.[122]
2 CONT * Cohen's music also appeared the following year in the 1966 NFB animated short Angel.
2 CONT * In 1968 Cohen performed on the BBC, both on the Julie Felix Show and in his own special, split into two thirty-minute shows. The first clip still exists, while the second is only available as a bootleg CD.[123]
2 CONT * Cohen makes a cameo appearance performing "The Stranger Song" in the Canadian film The Ernie Game (1968), which is based on the stories of Bernard Cole Spencer.
2 CONT * Cohen's music was popular among the directors of the New German Cinema, and appeared in Rainer Werner Fassbinder's Beware of a Holy Whore (1971) and Werner Herzog's Stroszek (1977).
2 CONT * Cohen's 1970 performance at the Isle of Wight Festival was filmed by Murray Lerner, but remained unreleased until 2009 when it was released as Leonard Cohen Live at the Isle of Wight 1970, and included Lerner's 2009 documentary 
2 CONC material.
2 CONT * Robert Altman's 1971 western McCabe and Mrs. Miller used three Cohen songs on its soundtrack, as well the instrumental parts of The Stranger Song performed by Cohen. Multiple record singles were released during the film's distri
2 CONC bution.[124]
2 CONT * In 1972, Cohen's European tour was filmed by British director Tony Palmer; the re-edited material appeared in 1974 under the title Bird on a Wire and exists only as a bootleg VHS tape. Palmer's authorised and restored film was r
2 CONC eleased in 2010.[125]
2 CONT * In October 1979, Cohen recorded a show for German TV show RockPop Special, available only as a bootleg. The show is often rebroadcast by the German satellite TV channel 3Sat.[126]
2 CONT * The Song of Leonard Cohen, a 1980 documentary by Harry Rasky, featuring Irving Layton, includes highlights from Cohen's 1979 European tour and tracks from the LP Recent Songs.
2 CONT * In 1983, Cohen wrote and starred in made-for-TV short musical film I am a Hotel, based on his songs. It won the Golden Rose award at the Rose d'Or television festival in Montreux in 1984.
2 CONT * In 1985, Cohen co-wrote and co-composed Night Magic (starring Carole Laure) with fellow Quebecker, Lewis Furey.
2 CONT * Cohen is subject of the BBC documentary Songs from the Life of Leonard Cohen, filmed during his 1988 tour.[127]
2 CONT * Cohen appeared as villain Francois Zolan in the "French Twist" episode of the American television series Miami Vice (season 2, episode 17), originally broadcast on 21 February 1986.
2 CONT * In 1988, Spanish TV station RTE televised the full version of Cohen's show in San Sebastian, 20 May, later aired in number of European countries and still rerun on the Spanish TV. It remains the only completely televised Cohen s
2 CONC how.
2 CONT * In 1989, Cohen gave his first major performance on US television on Austin City Limits[128] (recorded in November 1988). He performed for the show again in July 1993. Both programs have been often rebroadcast by the PBS.
2 CONT * "Everybody Knows" and "If It Be Your Will" play prominent parts in the 1990 film Pump Up the Volume
2 CONT * Three of Cohen's songs from his album The Future ("Waiting for the Miracle", "Anthem", and "The Future") are used in Oliver Stone's 1994 film Natural Born Killers. Songs from this album have also appeared in the films Wonder Boy
2 CONC s (2000), starring Michael Douglas and The Life of David Gale (2003), starring Kevin Spacey.
2 CONT * In 1994, Cohen narrated NFB-produced documentary The Tibetan Book of the Dead.
2 CONT * Cohen's years at the Mt. Baldy Zen Centre were the subject of 1997 French documentary Leonard Cohen: Spring 96 by Armelle Brusq, released on DVD in 2009.
2 CONT * In 2001, Cohen's song "Hallelujah" was used on the soundtrack of Shrek, in a version by Rufus Wainwright.
2 CONT * The Favourite Game (Le Jeu de l'ange), based on his novel of the same title, was released in Canada in 2003.
2 CONT * Leonard Cohen: I'm Your Man was released in 2006. It features a 2005 tribute concert to Cohen, "Came So Far For Beauty", held at the Sydney Opera House and produced by Hal Willner. The film, directed by Lian Lunson, has appearan
2 CONC ces by Nick Cave, Beth Orton, Antony of Antony and the Johnsons, Rufus and Martha Wainwright, and a performance of "Tower of Song" by Cohen and U2. The film also features Cohen recalling significant parts of his life and career.
2 CONT * Cohen is the subject of a two-part documentary, Leonard Cohen: Under Review 1934–1977 (2007) and Leonard Cohen: Under Review 1978–2006 (2008), available separately on DVD.
2 CONT * In 2009, the song First We Take Manhattan, that Cohen originally wrote for Jennifer Warnes in 1987, but then he recorded a version himself and released it as a single in 1988, was used during the end credits to the film Watchmen
2 CONC .
2 CONT * In the 2011 Malayalam film Pranayam, the paraplegic character portrayed by Mohanlal is an ardent fan of Cohen. The song, "I'm Your Man" is included in the film.
2 CONT
