0 @N6000000002403891611@ NOTE My Zadie Mordecai Yehuda Garmaise, my mother's father, also known by the name Mordecai Zawels, was considered by all to be a good and righteous man. He was careful to keep every mitzva, light as well as serious, he was easygoing w
1 CONC ith people and received everyone in a cheerful manner. However he was not a profound scholar, nor was he of the ordained elite, but he was conversant with the traditional books, and each morning he would study by himself a few cha
1 CONC pters of Mishna before going out to do business in the city, and every evening on his .return he would study the weekly portion with Rashi IS commentary. He knew the Shulchan Aruch practically by memory. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a man of many virtues, hospitable to guests like the patriarch Abraham in his time. Since Shuki was at the crossroads between Vilkevishk and Verzhbelova, not a day would pass that he did not accomodate one or more guests in
1 CONC  his house, and not only did he provide them with one or two meals and a place to spend the night, but he would furnish them with a regular gift in accordance with God's bounty to him.
1 CONT
1 CONT My grandfather had a very melodious voice, and the first time I heard him sing the Friday night Zemirot, it seemed to me that a chorus of heavenly angels was singing with him. I experienced a taste of Paradise. All my life since, 
1 CONC when I sing the zemirot of Friday night and Shabbat afternoon, I have used the melody which I learned from my Zadie Reb Mordecai Yehuda, and they are as sweet as honey in my mouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the first day that I came to live with Zadie, he set aside times for me to study Torah. And this is what he said to me: "Hershel, I have brought you here for two reasons, The first reason is, when I saw your mother ill and we
1 CONC ak, I thought that by taking you with me to spend a few months, I would make it easier on her. The second is that I am your Zadie, and it is my desire that my favorite grandson should learn what I have to teach him, as it is writt
1 CONC en in the Torah, "You shall teach them to your children," and'grandchildren are as children.  I have therefore decided that starting tomorrow we shall be study partners, and we shall study together for one hour every day right aft
1 CONC er dinner."
